# cat — cat.pdxdoc
#
# Long-form documentation, `doc cat` back-end, invariant I7 §2 in
# design/tooling/plan.md (paideia-os): "the man-equivalent. Longer
# prose, cross-references, historical context ('this replaces POSIX
# `<x>`; the differences are…')".
#
# This is the FIRST .pdxdoc file to ship in the R50 wave, and cat is
# the first tool to reach M5, so the concrete shape below is the
# working reference for the other R49/R50 tools' M5-001 issues (per
# r49-r50-plan.md §5.5). The doc.M1-002 parser (issue in the doc
# repo) reads THIS shape.
#
# Format sketch (subject to doc.M1-002 refinement — every field below
# is text-line-oriented so the parser can be a byte-scanner without
# an intermediate lexer):
#
#   .DOC <tool-name> <version>
#   .META <key>: <value>            (one per line; RFC-2822 style)
#   .END-META
#   .SECTION <title>                (opens a section; § = "chapter")
#     free prose lines
#     .XREF <target>                (cross-ref to <tool>#section or url)
#     .CODE                         (opens a code block)
#       lines
#     .END-CODE
#     .POSIX-DIFF                   (opens a POSIX-difference callout)
#       lines
#     .END-POSIX-DIFF
#   .END-SECTION
#   .END-DOC
#
# The `doc` reader renders .SECTION headings with SGR bold, .CODE with
# a monospace SGR, and .POSIX-DIFF with an inline callout (doc.M2-004
# in r49-r50-plan.md §5.3). At M1 doc renders everything as plain
# text (doc.M1-003). The semantic-pipe layer emits one PdxDocEntry
# record per .SECTION (doc.M3-001).

.DOC cat 1.0.0
.META tool:         cat
.META version:      1.0.0
.META wave:         R50
.META category:     A
.META posix_ancestor: cat
.META manifest:     manifest.pdxsig
.META caps_decl:    caps.decl
.META source_repo:  github.com/paideia-os/cat
.META design_ref:   design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §5.5 (paideia-os)
.META license:      MIT
.END-META

.SECTION Name
`cat` — concatenate files and stream their bytes to stdout, with
optional line-numbering, non-printable rendering, and a semantic-pipe
layer that forwards typed records unchanged when the source file
declares a schema.
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Synopsis
.CODE
cat [-n|-A|--schema] [<file>...]
.END-CODE

With one or more `<file>` arguments, `cat` reads each file in the
order given and writes its bytes to the invoker's terminal (stdout,
bound to KIND_TTY at exec). With zero positional arguments, `cat`
reads its stdin instead (a KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT bound by the invoking
shell's pipeline mint per shell.M2-002).
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Description
`cat` is a byte pump with three concurrent output layers:

1. Rendered text — file bytes go verbatim to KIND_TTY. With `-n`,
   each output line is prefixed by its right-justified 6-column line
   number followed by a tab. With `-A`, non-printable bytes render
   as `^X` / `M-x` / `$` line-end markers matching the GNU
   `cat -A = -vET` convention.
2. Typed schema stream — if the source file's .pdxfs metadata
   declares a schema (schema hash present in the file's cap
   descriptor sidecar), `cat` streams that schema's records on the
   semantic-pipe layer unchanged (schema-typed passthrough,
   invariant D2 in design/tooling/plan.md; implemented at M3-001).
   If no schema is declared, `cat` emits `RawByteChunk[]` records
   (offset + bytes) at 64 KiB granularity (M3-002).
3. Audit tail — every file read is journaled as a
   `FileReadRecord` via libpdx-audit before the first byte crosses
   into the sink (audit-first invariant D3; implemented at M3-003).

Streaming is invariant: no file is ever fully buffered. The working
set is bounded by a single 64 KiB chunk buffer regardless of file
size (M2-003; regression test at M4-003).
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Flags
`--version` prints the tool's own build version and exits 0; it is
handled directly in `_start` before argv reaches the parser, so it
composes with nothing else on the command line (`cat --version`
only — extra arguments fall through to the normal parser and are
usage errors). There is no `--help`: long-form documentation is a
`doc cat` query against this file, not a second copy of the flag
table compiled into the binary (ENH-005, paideia-os/cat #20).

The tool-specific flags are:

.CODE
  -n          Prefix each output line with a right-justified 6-column
              line number followed by a tab. Numbering starts at 1
              and is invocation-global (spans across multiple file
              arguments).

  -A          Render non-printable bytes as visible escapes:
                control chars 0x00..0x1F, 0x7F  → ^X (X = c ^ 0x40)
                high-bit chars 0x80..0xFF       → M-x
                newline                         → $\n
              Convenience alias equivalent to POSIX `cat -vET`.

  --schema    Force schema-typed passthrough even when the input file
              carries no schema hash. When the file's .pdxfs metadata
              declares no schema, `cat` does NOT refuse — it falls
              back to `RawByteChunk@0.1` emission so a downstream
              consumer can still window the stream by byte range.
              (Corrected per ENH-005: this flag never exits non-zero
              on its own account; the previous "refuses (exit 2) if
              the file has no readable .pdxfs metadata" text
              contradicted the fallback described in `.SECTION
              Semantic pipe layout` below and was never what the
              implementation did.)

  --version   Print "cat (paideia-os) 1.0.0" and exit 0. Only
              recognized as the sole argument (argc == 2); combined
              with any other flag or positional it is treated as an
              unrecognized long flag by the normal parser (exit 2).
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Capabilities
The exec-time capability manifest is defined in `caps.decl`.

.CODE
requires:
  - KIND_USER
  - KIND_TTY(write)
  - KIND_PDXFS_FILE(read, <arg-path>)     # one cap per file arg
  - KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT
.END-CODE

Per-argument narrowing: `cat a b c` receives three distinct
KIND_PDXFS_FILE(read) caps — one for each of a, b, c. No wildcard
subtree is ever granted. The shell constructs these from the invoker's
cap environment via libpdx-cap M2's cap_pack with narrow_rights.

.XREF caps#per-argument-narrowing
.XREF plan.md#I6-cap-manifest
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Exit codes
`cat` follows invariant I4 (collapsed exit-code vocabulary) in
design/tooling/plan.md §4.2:

.CODE
  0    every file read and rendered successfully
  2    usage error   (missing file, unknown flag, name too long)
  3    system error  (audit journal unreachable, sink overflow,
                      file-read I/O error)
  4    cap denied    (no read cap for a named file's path)
.END-CODE

Distinct codes at the assertion layer let a shell script (or a test
harness) decode which failure class tripped without inspecting logs.
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Semantic pipe layout
`cat` emits two record shapes on stdout's semantic-pipe layer,
selected per file:

.CODE
  # Schema-typed passthrough (M3-001)
  frame RecordFrame {
      schema_hash: [u8; 32],     # from file's .pdxfs cap-desc sidecar
      body_len:    u32,
      body:        [u8; body_len],
  }

  # Schemaless RawByteChunk (M3-002)
  schema RawByteChunk@0.1 {
      offset: u64,               # byte offset in source file
      bytes:  [u8],              # up to CHUNK_MAX = 65536 bytes
  }
.END-CODE

The passthrough case is byte-exact: the wire frame body is the file
body, no re-encoding, no schema translation. This is the D2 literal
that makes `cat known-typed-file | downstream-consumer` work with
zero schema-aware code in the downstream consumer beyond schema-hash
recognition.

.XREF plan.md#D2-composition-semantic-first
.XREF libpdx-semantic-pipe.pdxdoc#passthrough
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Differences from POSIX cat

.POSIX-DIFF
POSIX cat has no notion of typed streams. GNU/BSD cat streams
opaque bytes always. Paideia cat streams bytes AND, when the source
file's .pdxfs metadata declares a schema, forwards typed records
unchanged on the semantic-pipe layer. A downstream consumer that
knows the schema reads records; one that does not reads bytes. Both
work.
.END-POSIX-DIFF

.POSIX-DIFF
POSIX cat has no capability manifest. Paideia cat receives EXACTLY
the caps declared in caps.decl and no more; extra caps are refused
by the shell at exec (libpdx-cap cap_manifest_verify). This means
`cat /etc/shadow` on Paideia refuses with exit 4 if the invoking
shell does not hold a KIND_PDXFS_FILE(read, /etc/shadow) cap to
narrow forward — whereas POSIX cat refuses only on POSIX file-mode
bits, which are advisory relative to the kernel.
.END-POSIX-DIFF

.POSIX-DIFF
POSIX cat has no audit tail. Paideia cat journals a FileReadRecord
via libpdx-audit before the first byte crosses into the sink. A
downstream `journal-x` (audit explorer, plan.md §5 category E) can
answer "who read this file, at what time, from what shell session,
with what argv" without extra tooling.
.END-POSIX-DIFF

.POSIX-DIFF
POSIX cat's -A flag (or -vET on strict POSIX) is spelled the same,
same semantics. The -n flag is spelled the same, same semantics. The
--schema flag is Paideia-native (no POSIX analog).
.END-POSIX-DIFF

.POSIX-DIFF
POSIX cat's -u (unbuffered) flag has no analog. Paideia cat is
always streaming — the 64 KiB chunk buffer at src/file_read.pdx is
the ONLY working-set the tool ever holds. `-u` would be a no-op.
.END-POSIX-DIFF

.POSIX-DIFF
POSIX cat's -b (number non-blank lines only) is not implemented at
v1.0. The distinction is rarely worth the complexity — `cat -n` for
all lines, or pipe through a `grep -n` for the non-blank subset.
Filed as a candidate v1.1 addition if user demand appears.
.END-POSIX-DIFF
.END-SECTION

.SECTION Examples
.CODE
# Single file to terminal:
cat notes.md

# Multi-file concatenation, order-preserving:
cat header.pdx body.pdx footer.pdx

# Line-numbered:
cat -n build.log

# Non-printable-visible (great for hex-tainted files):
cat -A binary.dump

# Schema-typed passthrough (source has a schema hash):
cat data.records | table

# Stdin passthrough through a shell pipeline (audit NOT fired):
grep foo | cat | wc -l
.END-CODE
.END-SECTION

.SECTION See also

.XREF pkg.pdxdoc                    # install / verify signed packages
.XREF shell.pdxdoc#pipeline         # how | mints IPC_ENDPOINT frames
.XREF doc.pdxdoc                    # the .pdxdoc reader itself
.XREF ls.pdxdoc                     # sibling coreutil, category A
.XREF libpdx-audit.pdxdoc#FileReadRecord
.XREF libpdx-semantic-pipe.pdxdoc#passthrough
.XREF plan.md#I4-exit-codes         # in paideia-os design/tooling/
.XREF r49-r50-plan.md#5.5           # cat's milestone breakdown
.END-SECTION

.SECTION History
Landed as the second R50 tool to reach v1.0 (after pkg-bootstrap;
pkg proper still en route via §5.1). Milestone rollup at STATUS.md;
per-issue implementation notes at .plans/.

M1 (2026-08-21) scaffolded the repo + argv surface + first-runnable
KIND_TTY output. M2 landed multi-file streaming + `-n` / `-A` +
stdin passthrough. M3 wired the semantic-pipe layer (schema-typed +
schemaless) and the audit-first invariant. M4 landed four
correctness tests covering argv-order preservation, schema
passthrough, bounded-streaming for large files, and stdin piping.
M5 (this release) ships the dual-signed manifest, the .pdxdoc file
you are reading, and the mirror-push manifest to pkgs.paideia-os
staging.

Six of nine source modules — FileRead, StdinSource, TtySink,
PipeOut, FileSchema, AuditStub — carry test-seedable stubs that
stand in for kernel substrates not yet landed at HEAD
(KIND_PDXFS_FILE from R42; shell.M2 pipeline mint;
svc.audit-journal broker from R49-PREP-006). The stubs remain in
place at v1.0 by design: the M4 correctness assertions are on
observable I/O effects, not on which side of the stub boundary the
byte crossed. When the real substrates wire in, the seed helpers
retire, the .so bindings replace the stubs, and cat is rebuilt with
no source changes on its dispatcher or argv or render layers.
.END-SECTION

.END-DOC
